# Copy to .env and adjust. .env is gitignored and must stay that way.
#
# All configuration comes from environment variables — no hardcoded hosts, keys, or paths
# anywhere in the tree. See CLAUDE.md.
#
# Variables land here with the code that reads them, rather than being guessed at in advance.
# Settled so far: the database, and the landing page in web/.

# --- db/setup-local.sh reads these three ---------------------------------------------------
# The superuser is used once, to create the roles and the database. Nothing else uses it.
PGSUPERUSER=postgres
PGDATABASE=recruitcopilot

# Required, with no default anywhere. Generate one — do not invent a memorable string, and do
# not copy this placeholder, because whatever ships in this file ships to everyone:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Then use the same value in the two URLs below.
RCP_LOCAL_PASSWORD=__GENERATE_ME__

# --- Two connections, deliberately -------------------------------------------------------
# Row-level security is bypassed by a superuser and by the table owner alike, so the role the
# application connects as must be neither. rcp_app owns nothing and cannot bypass RLS; every
# query it makes is filtered by the policies. This split is the whole isolation guarantee —
# collapse these two URLs into one and the invariant tests still pass while proving nothing.
#
# rcp_owner runs migrations. It owns the schema and can create tables.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://rcp_owner:__GENERATE_ME__@localhost:5432/recruitcopilot

# rcp_app runs the application. Never use this role for migrations, and never use rcp_owner
# for anything a request touches.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://rcp_app:__GENERATE_ME__@localhost:5432/recruitcopilot

# The same rcp_app connection, under the name api/tests/test_invariants.py reads: the
# isolation tests open raw connections as the role the application actually uses, because
# proving isolation over the owner connection proves nothing. The test harness rewrites the
# database name to the test database at run time (api/conftest.py).
APP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://rcp_app:__GENERATE_ME__@localhost:5432/recruitcopilot

# --- api/ ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which connection Django uses is decided by context (api/config/settings.py): migrations
# and the test runner take MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL, everything a request touches takes
# DATABASE_URL. Generate the secret key the same way as the password:  openssl rand -hex 32
DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=__GENERATE_ME__
DJANGO_DEBUG=1

# --- web/ : hostnames ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The apex is canonical and the redirect host permanently redirects to it (ADR 0005). The
# rule lives in web/next.config.mjs because App Service has no hostname redirect of its own.
# Both unset locally, and the rule is then omitted rather than guessed at. Set both in the
# App Service configuration.
CANONICAL_HOST=
REDIRECT_HOST=

# --- web/ : the capture endpoint (Mailjet) -------------------------------------------------
# app/api/capture/route.ts subscribes each sign-up to a Mailjet contact list, in the same
# account that already sends for meanderhq.com. See ADR 0009 for why Mailjet rather than the
# provider-agnostic transport ADR 0007 described.
#
# Credentials are the same pair meander-backend uses (Account settings → REST API). They are
# account-wide, not per-list — treat the secret as a production credential and set it in App
# Service configuration, never here.
MAILJET_API_KEY=
MAILJET_SECRET_KEY=

# Two lists, never merged. Which form someone fills in is the signal Phase 0 exists to read,
# and a single list with a tag loses it the first time a bulk send goes out to "everyone".
# Create both in Mailjet (Contacts → Contact lists) and paste the numeric ids.
#
# Until the key, the secret and the relevant list id are all set, that form refuses sign-ups
# with a 503 and tells the visitor to write in. That is deliberate: accepting a sign-up and
# logging it to the App Service log stream is not storage, and a confirmation for something
# that was never recorded is the one outcome worse than the form being down.
MAILJET_LIST_COURSE=
MAILJET_LIST_SOFTWARE=

# The software form also sends agency_size and current_ats as Mailjet contact properties.
# BOTH MUST EXIST in the account first (Contacts → Properties, or the /contactmetadata API).
# Mailjet rejects the entire contact — not just the unknown field — when a property is not
# defined, which is how meander-backend lost a backfill to `esp_source`. The endpoint retries
# without properties rather than dropping the address, so an undefined property costs you the
# segmentation but never the sign-up, and logs loudly when it happens.

# Only for pointing the endpoint at a stub in a test — meander-backend hardcodes the same
# value as a module constant, which is why there is no such variable there. Leave it
# commented out; the endpoint defaults to https://api.mailjet.com/v3/REST.
# MAILJET_API_BASE=
